    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "Hash, range, or directory",
      "question": "Hash, range, or directory sharding: what does each one trade off?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Database sharding, question one. Hash, range, or directory sharding: what does each one trade off? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "<em>Hash</em>, <em>range</em> or <em>directory</em> sharding: what's the trade-off?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "With hash sharding, a lookup by user ID hashes the key, and lands on exactly one shard, and data spreads evenly. But a range query, all orders in May, has no idea where those rows live, so the router must scatter it to every shard. Range sharding fixes that: May sits on one shard. The catch is a hotspot: every new order lands in the newest range, so one shard takes all the writes. A directory maps each key to a shard in a lookup table. You can move any key anywhere, but every query pays an extra hop, and the directory must never go down.",
